    ":question/problem": "$\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=$",
    ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Here, the first factor is $4\\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}✓\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3=4\\times 3\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6\\cdot (-1) & 6\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0 \\\\ 6\\cdot (-1) & 6\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-1) & (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-1) & (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
